      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i just had to do a rumenotomy on one of my heifers because of a blockage. can u give me a list of what i should be looking for in terms of recovery and signs of infection over the next week? also what should i feed her once she starts acting normal again?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A rumenotomy, that's a serious procedure, but I'm sure your heifer is in good hands. You're probably thinking of her as a 'gobstopper' of a cow, with a blockage that's been causing her a world of trouble. \n\nNow, when it comes to recovery, you'll want to keep an eye out for signs of infection, such as discharge from the incision site, redness, swelling, or a foul odor. You might also notice that her appetite has decreased or that she's not producing enough milk. These are all potential warning signs that something's amiss.\n\nIn terms of specific signs, you should be looking for:\n\n- Increased redness or swelling around the incision site\n- Discharge or pus from the incision site\n- A decrease in appetite or water intake\n- Lethargy or depression\n- Abdominal pain or discomfort\n- Changes in milk production or quality\n\nAs for feeding, once your heifer is feeling better, you can start introducing her to a diet of high-quality hay and grains. You might want to consider a 'gob-stopper' of a grain mix, with a mix of oats, corn, and other nutrient-rich",
      "source": "target",
